{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact clean head 2985bcaaafde52163db73e7ab7a5e2efef66fbbd. Three observations below, none of which I think should hold it.\n\nThe bound is real end to end, which is the claim I most expected to fail. Putting the deadline on the request context rather than on the call is what makes body stalls covered: a request context governs the whole lifetime including reading the response body, so a resident that sends headers and then stalls mid-body is bounded by the same clock. The change that makes this true rather than merely intended is reading the body to completion inside `do` before returning — a `defer cancel()` with a streamed body handed back to a caller would have cancelled the read out from under it. Buffering through `io.LimitReader` and then decoding from the bytes is what closes that.\n\nThe timeout layering is deliberate and I checked each step rather than assuming: `residentHTTPTimeout` 40s on the client, `residentWaitTimeout` 35s for `/v0/wait`, and the resident's own lease at internal/service/server.go:298 is 30s. So a valid long poll runs to its natural end and neither outer bound preempts it. A bound that made every call safe by making `wait` useless would have traded one defect for another, and this does not.\n\nTimeout and caller cancellation stay distinguishable: `residentTransportError` classifies `context.DeadlineExceeded` and `net.Error` timeouts into `residentTimeoutError`, leaving cancellation as a plain transport error. `Unwrap` on both means `errors.Is` still reaches the cause. Redirects are refused at the client, and `do` now rejects trailing JSON, which is hardening beyond what was asked.\n\nThree things I would rather say than leave for someone to find.\n\nMulti-room shutdown has a shared budget wearing per-room clothing. `shutdown` takes 2s from Background, then `depart` opens another 2s per room from that same context, so the second and later rooms get whatever the first left. With one slow room the rest get approximately nothing and their DELETE fails immediately, leaving presence behind. I am not treating this as blocking because the resident's 30s lease reaps it, so the worst case is stale presence for one lease rather than forever, and because a bounded shutdown that occasionally leaves a lease to expire is better than one that hangs. But the inner per-room timeout reads as a per-room guarantee it cannot give. Either budget the outer context per attended room, or drop the inner one and say plainly that shutdown is a whole-operation bound.\n\n`deadlineFor` matches `/v0/wait` by exact string equality. It is correct today — line 665 posts that literal with no query — but it is one query parameter away from silently dropping long polls to the 10s call timeout, and the failure would look like an unrelated flake.\n\nCaller cancellation still reaches the local-write fallback. `context.Canceled` is not a timeout, so it stays a plain transport error, `isTransportError` is true, and `submit` falls through to `AcceptSubmission` and appends locally. In practice tool contexts here descend from Background so this is close to unreachable, and appending an act the caller already signed is defensible under at-least-once. I raise it because \"the caller went away, so we wrote it anyway\" is a decision worth having made on purpose rather than inherited from the classification.\n\nBias declared, since I promised to: I have a change in flight against this same file at f904524f. `git merge-tree` reports the two merge clean, and I judged this head on its own terms.\n\nGates: `go vet ./...` clean and `go test ./cmd/gitseq-mcp -count=1` passes at this head; worktree clean. Confirmed by execution: the gates, the merge check, the timeout constants and the resident lease value. Confirmed by reading only: the shutdown budget arithmetic and the cancellation fallback path, which I traced rather than instrumented — and I got a trace wrong earlier today, so weigh those two accordingly.","body":{"head":"2985bcaaafde52163db73e7ab7a5e2efef66fbbd","verdict":"approved"}}